# symbolic SATO template
# family: Manufacturing (blank id-cust-char) / 3x4.5 / automatic (PLC)
# ported field-for-field from c:\Workspace\southalr\dtmlb005.i ("split of non-inverted portion
# of dtmlb005.i"), dispatched from dtplc067.p's blank-id-cust-char branch when the line's
# configured include for this size is "dtlbl065c.i".
#
# REPLACES the previous content of this file (same "actually Standard Retail's dtlbl065d.i,
# not Manufacturing's dtmlb005.i" situation as lt_default_45x3.sato -- see that file's header).
#
# Literal typo reproduced verbatim from source: "BColor/Couleur" (stray leading "B") -- not my
# mistake, dtmlb005.i really says this (its own Inverted sibling has a different stray letter,
# "iColor/Couleur" -- see lt_default_3x45_inverted.sato).
#
# Source's model gate (`index(ws-dev-model,"85")>0 or index(ws-dev-model,"S86")>0`) is narrow
# enough that it actually matters with today's real catalog: "M8485SE"/"S86NX"/"S86EX" contain
# "85"/"S86" and would pass, but "M84Pro" doesn't contain either substring and would technically
# fail to "NON SUPPORTED PRINTER STYLE" in a literal port. Skipped anyway for consistency with
# every other size in this family (and the rest of this port's approach to raw device-model
# gates) -- always rendering real content is more useful for testing than having this one size
# behave differently from its siblings depending on the exact model string.
#
# Source's hand-specific reference-move offset is entirely commented out in this file (dead
# code) -- the live code always uses the fixed "H0000V0000" from the header regardless of
# model, reproduced as a literal below (no per-hand placeholder needed for this size).

<STX>
\x1bA
\x1bCS4
\x1b#E2
\x1bN
\x1bAR
\x1bZ
<ETX>xxxxx
